# .trivyignore.noruby — the suppression surface for the RUBY-FREE operator image.
#
# ── ★ THIS FILE IS SHRINKING ON PURPOSE. THAT IS THE POINT. ──────────────────
# It exists because the image bakes in terraform provider binaries, and those
# Go closures carry CVEs our own Rust does not. Its stated purpose since the day
# it was written is to reach zero — not by waiving more, but by no longer
# shipping the things being waived.
#
#   r349 (2026-08-27, first measurement)  190 findings · 49 ids · 8 providers
#   THIS COMMIT — `random` served natively 154 findings · 36 ids · 7 providers
#
# `terraform-provider-random` is gone from the image. magma now implements the
# `random` provider IN-PROCESS (`magma-provider-random`), registered at
# `pangea-operator/src/executor/magma.rs`'s `native_provider_router`. It was the
# largest single CVE contributor here — 36 of 190 findings, ahead of aws (11) —
# for a provider that makes NO network calls at all.
#
# ★ The 13 ids that left were EXACTLY the unfixable ones. Every id unique to
# random was a `golang.org/x/crypto` CVE whose FixedVersion is empty in the
# scanner's own data: no fixed release exists upstream, so no bump, patch or
# vendor override could ever have closed them. They were unwaivable-forever
# until we stopped shipping the binary. That is the argument for native
# providers in one line.
#
# ── HONEST ABOUT THESE NUMBERS ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# The 154/36 figures are DERIVED from r349's trivy JSON (artifact 9648954592)
# with the random target removed — a prediction, not a fresh measurement. The
# next release's gate is what confirms it. If the real count differs, trust the
# gate and re-derive this file from its artifact rather than editing counts by
# hand.
#
# ── WHAT IS STILL HERE, AND WHY IT IS WAIVED RATHER THAN FIXED ──────────────
#   * stdlib (13 ids) — fixed in Go 1.26.6. A property of the TOOLCHAIN
#     nixpkgs-security builds providers with, not of any provider's source, and
#     not patchable per-provider.
#   * kin-openapi — the 0.144.0 bump was built and CONFIRMED to break
#     terraform-provider-kubernetes' own source (CI run 30178928379). See
#     flake.nix's mirror comment. CVE-2026-73502 / CVE-2026-76905 are the SAME
#     finding `.trivyignore` carries as GHSA-jpcw-4wr7-c3vq / GHSA-xmrv-pmrh-hhx2,
#     re-identified upstream — not new.
#   * x/net, x/text, x/sys, x/mod, grpc, spdystream — transitive; each needs a
#     go.mod replace + vendorHash recompute per provider, the same class of
#     change that broke above.
#
# Every remaining group dies the same way random's did: the provider is
# implemented natively and the binary stops being shipped. Next by payoff ÷
# API complexity: porkbun (30) → rabbitmq (27) → datadog (23) → cloudflare (21)
# → github (14) → kubernetes (28, via engenho) → aws (11, the open question).
#
# ── THE BAR THAT HAS NOT MOVED ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# ZERO Ruby ids. `.trivyignore` carries 10 for the embedded gemset; waiving them
# here would suppress findings for an interpreter this image does not contain.
# They are not below, and that is verified by set intersection, not by eye.
#
# Regenerate rather than hand-edit: take the release's trivy JSON artifact and
# group its unique ids. Two ids appear under two packages and are listed under
# both, so the line count exceeds the id count by two.

